**************************************************  For example, I have a class called 'Person' and an instance of that class called 'p'. How can I verify that 'p' is an instance of 'Person'? In Python, you can use the built-in function `isinstance()` to check if an object is an instance of a class. The `isinstance()` function takes two arguments: the object you want to check and the class (or a tuple of classes) that you want to compare against.

Here's how you can use it in your case:

```python
class Person:
    pass

p = Person()

if isinstance(p, Person):
    print("p is an instance of Person")
else:
    print("p is not an instance of Person")
```

In this example, `isinstance(p, Person)` will return `True` because `p` is indeed an instance of the `Person` class. If `p` were an instance of a different class, or an instance of a subclass of `Person`, `isinstance()` would return `False` or `True` respectively, depending on the inheritance hierarchy.

If you want to check if an object is an instance of any of several classes, you can pass a tuple of classes as the second argument:

```python
if isinstance(p, (Person, AnotherClass)):
    # do something
```

This will return `True` if `p` is an instance of `Person` or `AnotherClass`.

Note that `isinstance()` is generally preferred over checking the type directly with `type()`, because it takes into account inheritance. For example, if you have a subclass `Student` of `Person`, `isinstance(Student(), Person)` will return `True`, whereas `type(Student()) == Person` will return `False`. So `isinstance()` is more flexible and robust in the face of inheritance. However, there are some cases where you might want to use `type()` instead, such as when you want to check for a specific type and not any of its subclasses. But in most cases, `isinstance()` is the right choice. 

Also, if you want to check if an object is exactly an instance of a class and not a subclass, you can use `type()`:

```python
if type(p) is Person:
    # do something
```

But again, this is less common and usually not 
